\section{Introduction}

The ongoing global pandemic caused by SARS-CoV-2, the virus responsible for COVID-19, has highlighted the critical need for understanding and predicting the genetic adaptations of the virus. Since its emergence, SARS-CoV-2 has undergone numerous genetic mutations, some of which have significantly increased its transmissibility and virulence. These mutations pose significant challenges for public health, as they can render existing vaccines and treatments less effective and necessitate the development of new interventions (Tegally et al., 2020). Therefore, the ability to predict which mutations will be significant is crucial for developing effective vaccines and public health policies.

Predictive modeling of viral adaptations is critical for public health. It can help anticipate the emergence of new variants, guide vaccine development, and inform public health policies. By identifying potential high-risk mutations, researchers and policymakers can take proactive measures to mitigate the impact of these variants. For example, early detection of mutations that enhance viral transmissibility can lead to more targeted surveillance and intervention strategies (Davies et al., 2021).

Despite the importance of predicting viral mutations, several challenges remain. The rapid evolution of the virus and the complexity of its genetic changes make it difficult to predict which mutations will be significant. Naive approaches, such as simple sequence alignment, fail to capture the nuanced interactions between mutations and their impact on viral function. Additionally, the lack of comprehensive and real-time data on viral sequences and their corresponding phenotypes makes it difficult to train robust predictive models (Korber et al., 2020).

Existing approaches often focus on specific mutations or use basic statistical methods to identify potential variants of concern (VOCs). However, these methods are limited in their ability to predict the functional impact of multiple mutations simultaneously. For instance, identifying a single mutation that increases transmissibility may not account for the combined effect of multiple mutations, which can significantly alter viral behavior (Korber et al., 2020).

To address these challenges, we propose a novel predictive modeling approach that integrates machine learning techniques with structural biology data. Our model aims to predict the functional impact of mutations on viral binding and transmission, thereby identifying potential high-risk mutations. By leveraging the structural context of the viral proteins, our approach can capture the complex interactions between mutations and their effects on viral function. This integration of structural biology with machine learning provides a more comprehensive and accurate prediction of viral adaptations.

